# Identifiers below are recorded AUTOMATICALLY on each release by
# .github/workflows/release-identifiers.yml — don't fill them in by hand.
#
# `doi:` is the CONCEPT DOI: it means "cite this project" and deliberately
# resolves to whatever version is latest. That floating behaviour is correct
# HERE and wrong in a nanopub — see scripts/set_release_identifiers.py.
#
# (This used to say "run /init-template again or edit by hand". /init-template
# deletes itself as its final step, so by the time a DOI existed the skill it
# named was gone and "edit by hand" was the only path left. Two repos built from
# this template then recorded the version DOI two different ways — one a
# structured identifier, one a prose comment. Hence the automation.)
#
# No DOI placeholder here: Zenodo reads this file when the release is created, and
# a literal "{{ZENODO_DOI}}" fails the CFF schema. The workflow writes `doi:` and
# the `identifiers:` list after Zenodo has minted them.
#
# On release, the workflow adds:
  #   - type: doi   value: <version DOI>  "Version DOI for vX.Y.Z — pins this exact release; cite this from nanopubs"
  #   - type: swh   value: swh:1:rev:<commit>;origin=<repo>  "Software Heritage ID of the vX.Y.Z source tree"
keywords:
- replication
- reproducibility
- FORRT
- nanopublication
- Science-Live
- natural regeneration
- tropical forest restoration
- random forest
- remote sensing
- spatial cross-validation
- HEALPix
- Colombia
abstract: >-
  Independent replication of the claim that the 30 m random-forest model of natural forest
  regeneration of Williams et al. (2024, Nature) has 87.9 % validation accuracy, tested in Colombia
  with open data and new code. Verdict: partially supported. The accuracy reproduces under random
  validation (0.887) but is lower under spatially blocked validation (0.812-0.869), for a model
  trained outside Colombia (0.767), and when predicting 2012-2024 regrowth from 2000-2012 with
  independent MapBiomas labels (0.717). The protocol follows the robustness checks suggested in
  The Unjournal's evaluation of the paper. Version 1.1.0 adds the prediction of every 30 m pixel of
  Colombia, aggregated conservatively to HEALPix (WGS84) and archived as a GRID4EARTH Zarr dataset
  (doi:10.5281/zenodo.23138775), and shows that the blocked accuracies do not depend on the blocking
  grid (HEALPix on WGS84 or sphere, latitude/longitude squares). Version 1.1.1 fixes the Docker
  image build (git added for a GitHub-pinned dependency); the source is otherwise identical to 1.1.0.
